  "id": "frottola",
  "name": "Frottola (north Italian court song, c. 1500)",
  "family": "classical",
  "lineage": "Italian courtly secular song of about 1470 to 1530, cultivated above all at the Mantuan court of Isabella d'Este by composers such as Marchetto Cara and Bartolomeo Tromboncino and printed by Ottaviano Petrucci in Venice in a series of frottola books from 1504 to 1514. Set to light poetic forms such as the barzelletta and strambotto, usually in four parts with the tune in the top voice, it was sung by a vocal ensemble or by a solo singer with the lower parts played on lute or viols, and gave way to the madrigal in the 1520s and 1530s.",
